## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a tiny 3-valued solver: propagation, clash detection, and controlled backtracking while preserving non-boolean placeholders.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued logic propagation
- Forward-checking and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking with Unknowns to preserve flexibility

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vars, constraints) {
  // vars: {S1: T|F|U, S2: T|F|U, S3: T|F|U}
  // constraints: list of functions returning {changed, ok}
  // Minimal sketch for illustration
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const c of constraints) {
      const res = c(vars);
      if (res.changed) changed = true;
      if (!res.ok) return {ok: false};
    }
  }
  return {ok: true, vars};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply public announcements to epistemic models and design a practical solver that scales with model size.

## Key Concepts
- Kripke models and accessibility relations
- Public announcements and product update
- Knowledge operator Ki and post-update evaluation
- Linear-time propagation and fixpoint reasoning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal representation and product update sketch
function productUpdate(W, relations, V, pSet) {
  const Wp = W.filter(w => pSet.has(w));
  const Rp = {};
  for (const [AG, rel] of Object.entries(relations)) {
    Rp[AG] = new Map();
    for (const w of Wp) {
      Rp[AG].set(w, rel.get(w).filter(v => pSet.has(v)));
    }
  }
  return { W: Wp, relations: Rp };
}

function knows(pSet, w, agentRel) {
  const targets = agentRel.get(w) || [];
  return targets.every(u => pSet.has(u));
}
``` 

